{
  "title": "Moving Checklist: Printable Timeline Built Around Your Move",
  "metaDescription": "Build a printable moving checklist matched to your move, from eight weeks out to your first month, then print it or save it as a Portable Document Format (PDF) file.",
  "h1": "Moving Checklist: Your Timeline from Eight Weeks Out to the First Month",
  "introText": "A move runs on a seven-stage countdown that starts about eight weeks out. Eight weeks out you set the budget and book movers or a truck. Four to six weeks out you sort belongings and gather records. Two weeks out you change your address and schedule utilities. The last week you pack and confirm, then come moving day, a first week of setup and a first month of license, voter and tax paperwork. Which specific tasks apply depends on whether you rent or own at each address, how far you travel, who transports your goods, and whether pets or kids accompany you.\n\nThe tool on this page builds that list from your answers. You enter where you live now, where you are going, your travel distance, your moving method, season, and whether you are bringing children, pets, or a vehicle. Pick the state you're moving to, and the four Local conditions options (Winters reach freezing, Hurricane-prone coast, Wildfire area, Earthquake area) are pre-set for that state. The presets are a starting point, not a statement about every town, so turn on any condition that applies at either home. Items that do not match your situation drop off the worksheet automatically. When you finish selecting options, click the Print or save as PDF button to print only the checklist, with Name, Date and Property address lines.",
  "toolHeading": "Build your moving checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Select your move details to generate a customized countdown worksheet.",
  "howItWorks": "Eight weeks out, you set a moving budget covering transport, packing supplies, travel, and any overlapping housing costs, while securing your moving date. Six to four weeks out, you sort household items into keep, sell, donate, or toss piles, document belongings with photographs, and collect medical or school records. Two weeks out, you change your address with USPS and the IRS, schedule utilities to stop and start, and line up insurance. The final week centers on packing essentials and confirming arrival windows. On moving day you photograph the meter readings and walk through every room before you leave. In the first week you test the smoke and carbon monoxide alarms and find the water shutoff and breaker panel. The first month covers your driver's license, voter registration and moving-expense tax questions.\n\nYour housing arrangement at both ends of the move reshapes your required tasks more than almost any other choice. If you rent now, the checklist adds written notice by the date your lease requires, a move-out walkthrough with your landlord and your forwarding address in writing. Your state (and sometimes your city) sets the deadline for returning the deposit; the [Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD)](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights) keeps a page of tenant rights by state. If you're leaving a rental or moving into one, the checklist links to our [move-in / move-out checklist](/checklists/move-in-move-out-checklist/) for the condition record and our [renting checklist](/checklists/renting-checklist/) for the new lease; first-time renters can also use the [first apartment checklist](/checklists/first-apartment-checklist/). If you're selling, the checklist lines up the sale with your moving date, keeps homeowners insurance on the old home until the sale closes and links to the [home selling checklist](/checklists/home-selling-checklist/). If you're buying, it lines up your closing with your move-out date and links to the [home buying checklist](/checklists/home-buying-checklist/) and the [final walkthrough checklist](/checklists/final-walkthrough-checklist/). If you own the home you're leaving and are keeping it, it asks whether the home will be rented out or sit empty, tells you to inform your insurer, and links to the [landlord checklist](/checklists/landlord-checklist/). Hiring movers adds written estimates from at least three movers and a check of each company's license or registration. A rental truck adds reserving the truck, a dolly, pads and straps, and asking your auto insurer and card issuer whether they cover it. A portable container adds deciding where it sits at each home, and a truck or container may need a street parking permit.\n\nDistance changes your regulatory protections and administrative paperwork. When your belongings cross a state line, federal rules from the [Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA)](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/protect-your-move) apply. Before you hire an interstate mover, look the company up by name, USDOT number or MC number to see its registration and complaint history, and ask whether it is the mover or a broker; [FMCSA's moving FAQ](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/protect-your-move/faq) explains that a broker books your move with a moving company and has no trucks. Federal regulations require interstate movers to provide the \"Your Rights and Responsibilities When You Move\" booklet and the \"Ready to Move\" brochure. Choose your mover's [liability option](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/consumer-protection/protect-your-move/are-you-moving/liability-protection) in writing: Full Value Protection places responsibility for replacement value on the carrier, whereas Released Value costs nothing extra but caps recovery at no more than 60 cents per pound per article (requiring a signed statement on the bill of lading). For an interstate move, you have nine months from delivery to submit a written damage claim. Moves inside one state follow that state's rules instead. A move to another state also adds a new driver's license and car registration, by deadlines the new state sets, and a question for a tax preparer about whether you'll file part-year returns in both states.\n\nFile your official change of address directly through the [United States Postal Service (USPS)](https://www.usps.com/manage/forward.htm). [USA.gov's change-of-address page](https://www.usa.gov/change-address) warns that look-alike sites charge far more than the official USPS service. File an address change with the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) using [IRS Form 8822](https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8822). If you receive Social Security or VA benefits, update your address with that agency too, along with your bank, credit cards, employer payroll and insurers. Update your voting record through [Vote.gov](https://vote.gov/). Under [IRS Topic 455](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc455), the federal deduction for moving expenses is restricted to active-duty military members relocating under military orders, with eligibility extending to certain intelligence community personnel starting in 2026.\n\nKids, pets, the season and your local conditions each add their own items. With kids, the checklist adds asking the new school district how to enroll, requesting school records, arranging childcare for moving day and finishing enrollment in the first week. With a pet, it adds vet records and refills, a travel bag with food and medication and a microchip update; on a move to another state, it adds asking your vet what the new state requires, such as a health certificate or rabies record. A summer move adds booking movers or the truck as soon as your date is set and carrying heat-sensitive items such as medications and electronics in your car. A winter move with Winters reach freezing turned on adds clearing snow and ice at both homes and keeping liquids, paint and houseplants out of an unheated truck or container. Hurricane-prone coast adds a backup date and evacuation routes for a spring-to-fall move and your evacuation zone at the new address ([Ready.gov: Hurricanes](https://www.ready.gov/hurricanes)). Wildfire area adds watching fire and smoke conditions before the move and signing up for local emergency alerts ([Ready.gov: Wildfires](https://www.ready.gov/wildfires)). Earthquake area adds strapping tall furniture and the water heater to the wall ([Ready.gov: Earthquakes](https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes)). In the first week, test the smoke and carbon monoxide alarms. The [National Fire Protection Association (NFPA)](https://www.nfpa.org/education-and-research/home-fire-safety/smoke-alarms) recommends smoke alarms inside every bedroom, outside each sleeping area and on every level, tested monthly.\n\nBuild your moving checklist as soon as your target move date is established, print a physical copy, and work through the requirements stage by stage. If you're hiring movers for a move to another state, check the company's [FMCSA registration](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/protect-your-move/faq) and choose your liability option in writing before anything else.",
  "workedExample": "In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist's rules for two example moves to count how many items each one keeps. The full master library contains 88 distinct items arranged across seven chronological stages. Exactly 26 core items apply to every relocation, while 62 appear only when one of your answers switches them on.\n\nScenario A is a renter moving across town in Chicago, Illinois to another rental, hiring movers, in summer, with no kids or pets: 46 of 88 items. It adds the lease notice, the move-out walkthrough and forwarding address, the deposit follow-up, renters insurance, recording the new rental's condition and checking the state's rules for in-state movers. It also adds the summer items and drops every interstate-mover, license-transfer, truck, kids and pet item. Scenario B is a homeowner selling in Ohio and buying in Arizona, hiring interstate movers, in winter, with kids and a dog: 63 of 88 items. It adds the FMCSA registration lookup, the federal booklet, the liability choice, the nine-month claim window, the home sale and purchase hand-offs, the new-state driver's license, car registration and part-year tax question, school and pet items, and the winter freezing items. It drops the rental items and the summer items.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Hiring an interstate mover without verifying their USDOT or MC registration number on the official FMCSA database.",
    "Filing your change of address on a look-alike site, which USA.gov warns charges far more than the official USPS service.",
    "Failing to choose your mover's liability option in writing before transport.",
    "Packing hazardous materials without telling the mover, which can limit the mover's liability.",
    "Assuming moving expenses are deductible, when IRS Topic 455 limits the federal deduction to active-duty military moving under orders and, from 2026, certain intelligence community employees.",
    "Missing state motor vehicle agency deadlines for transferring your driver's license and vehicle registration after crossing state lines."
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What should be on a moving checklist?",
      "answer": "A moving checklist should contain tasks organized into a timeline covering budgeting, booking transport, sorting personal belongings, and gathering personal records. It must include utility shutoffs and connections, address updates across government agencies, and moving-day walkthroughs. The list also needs post-move tasks like testing smoke alarms and updating vehicle registrations."
    },
    {
      "question": "How far in advance should I start planning a move?",
      "answer": "Our moving checklist tool organizes planning across an eight-week countdown starting with budgeting and reserving transportation. Starting your preparations earlier can provide greater flexibility when selecting moving dates and movers."
    },
    {
      "question": "Who do I need to notify when I move?",
      "answer": "Notify the USPS by submitting an official change of address, and update records with the IRS using Form 8822. If applicable, inform the Social Security Administration, Veterans Affairs, and USCIS. You must also notify your state motor vehicle agency, election officials via Vote.gov, financial institutions, insurance providers, and employer payroll."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do I check if an interstate moving company is legitimate?",
      "answer": "Look the company up by name, USDOT number or MC number on [FMCSA's Protect Your Move site](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/protect-your-move/faq) to see its registration and complaint history. Ask whether the business operates as an actual carrier with its own fleet or acts as a broker arranging service. [FMCSA's red flags](https://www.fmcsa.dot.gov/protect-your-move/red-flags) include an estimate given by phone or online without seeing your belongings, a demand for cash or a large deposit before the move, a request to sign blank documents, and a website with no local address or registration information."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the difference between Full Value Protection and Released Value?",
      "answer": "Full Value Protection makes the mover responsible for the replacement value of lost or damaged goods, and it applies unless you sign a statement on the bill of lading choosing Released Value. Released Value comes at no additional cost but caps compensation at no more than 60 cents per pound per article."
    },
